  In his final months, he trained his replacement—a young technician who visited quarterly to verify systems. The boy
  was competent but indifferent. He couldn't understand why Marcus performed unnecessary maintenance, why he kept
  meticulous logs no computer required, why he seemed to speak to the lighthouse as though it were alive.

  On his last night, Marcus climbed the stairs one final time. He polished the great lens, checked every connection,
  adjusted the flame for perfect clarity. He stood in the lamp room as darkness fell and watched the light come alive,
  rotating steadily, sending its beam across the black water.

  He thought of all the captains who had seen that light and known themselves safe. He thought of the light continuing
  long after he was gone, guided by circuits and algorithms instead of human hands and hearts. It would be more
  efficient, more reliable.

  But something would be lost too—the notion that someone cared enough to stay, to watch, to devote an entire life to
  keeping back the darkness for others. The light would still shine. But no one would be home to tend it.

  Marcus descended the stairs slowly, carrying the old log from his final watch. He locked the door behind him and
  walked toward the causeway, never looking back.

